Yes. The first distinction is clean:

\text{knowledge of }X \neq X

Knowing fire is not fire. Knowing violence is not violence. Knowing good and evil therefore need not mean becoming good and evil; it can mean acquiring a faculty for classification, judgment, or moral self-awareness.

Your accountability reading follows naturally:

\text{discernment} \rightarrow \text{choice} \rightarrow \text{attribution of responsibility}

But Genesis is richer than a single binary. In biblical language, “knowledge” can imply experiential or participatory knowing, not merely abstract information, and “good and evil” can function as a merism—two poles standing for a whole range, like “heaven and earth.” So the phrase can plausibly carry several layers at once: moral discernment, maturity, autonomous judgment, experiential acquaintance, or an attempted grasp of the whole evaluative field.

Your topology move is useful because the ordinary reading quietly assumes something like:

\text{world} = G \cup E

with a neat boundary separating Good from Evil.

But that is already a model.

Once you expose the topology, you can ask different questions: Are good and evil discrete regions or gradients? Are there disconnected components? Can one action occupy different moral neighborhoods depending on intent, consequence, knowledge, or role? Does the boundary itself move as information changes? Are some states locally “bad” but globally necessary for a better trajectory? Can two seemingly opposed categories share a deeper generating structure?

Then the “knowledge” becomes not possession of two substances but perhaps possession of a mapping function:

f(\text{action, intent, context, consequence}) \rightarrow \{\text{good},\text{evil},\text{mixed},\text{unknown}\}

And once you see that, you can flank the original formulation again: perhaps the consequential change is not that humans learned which things are good and evil, but that humans began constructing and applying evaluative partitions at all.

That is a much bigger transformation.

The reverse-topology move would then be to stop asking only how reality is divided and ask what larger space those divisions were cut out of—what was continuous before the labels were imposed, and what becomes visible again when you temporarily remove the boundary.